.Dd $Mdocdate: July 8 2026 $
.Dt HPN3SCP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hpn3scp
.Nd guided third-party (direct source-to-target) file copy for HPN-SSH
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm hpn3scp
.Op Fl 46AVZrv
.Op Fl j Ar streams
.Op Fl o Ar ssh_option
.Op Fl X Ar sftp_option
.Op Fl Y Ar switches
.Ar source
.Ar target
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
copies files directly between two remote hosts \(en the
.Ar source
and the
.Ar target
\(en without the data passing through the local machine.
It is a front end for the
.Xr hpnscp 1
.Fl R
third-party transport: it arranges the pieces that a direct
remote-to-remote transfer needs \(en target host-key trust, a
source-to-target authentication check, and live progress \(en then
launches the transfer and relays its status.
.Pp
Three hosts take part:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It the launcher
The machine where you run
.Nm .
It orchestrates the transfer but never carries the file data.
.It the source
The host named by
.Ar source ,
which reads the files and connects onward to the target.
The
.Nm
launcher reaches it over
.Xr hpnssh 1
and runs
.Xr hpnscp 1
there.
.It the target
The host named by
.Ar target ,
which the source writes to.
.El
.Pp
Because the data flows source-to-target directly, the source must be able
to reach and authenticate to the target on its own.
How that authentication is arranged is described under
.Sx AUTHENTICATION .
.Pp
The
.Ar source
and
.Ar target
are each given as
.Sm off
.Oo Ar user No @ Oc Ar host : Op Ar path
.Sm on
or as an
.Ar scp://
URI
.Po
.Sm off
.No scp:// Oo Ar user No @ Oc Ar host Op : Ar port Oo / Ar path Oc
.Sm on
.Pc .
A missing
.Ar path
means the remote login directory; a missing
.Ar port
uses the default from
.Xr hpnssh_config 5 .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 4
Use IPv4 addresses only.
The address family applies to every connection
.Nm
makes: the target host-key scan, the authentication preflight, and the
transfer itself.
.It Fl 6
Use IPv6 addresses only.
.It Fl A
Forward the local
.Xr hpnssh-agent 1
to the source so it can authenticate to the target with your keys.
This is an explicit opt-in for the case where the source has no key of
its own on the target; see
.Sx AUTHENTICATION
for the trade-off it carries.
.It Fl j Ar streams
Use
.Ar streams
parallel data streams for the transfer
.Pq default 1 .
Larger values raise throughput on high-bandwidth, high-latency paths.
.It Fl o Ar ssh_option
Pass an
.Xr hpnssh 1
option, in the format used in
.Xr hpnssh_config 5 ,
to the source-to-target connection.
May be repeated.
.Pp
The
.Cm TransferLog Ns Op = Ns Ar path
option is consumed by
.Nm
itself: the per-file log is written on THIS host (default
.Pa ./hpnssh-transfer.log ) ,
built from the source's per-file status stream, with remote file names
percent-encoded before they are written.
See the
.Fl o
entry in
.Xr hpnscp 1
for the format.
.It Fl r
Copy directories recursively.
.It Fl v
Raise the verbosity.
May be repeated for more detail; passed through to the underlying
programs.
.It Fl V
Verify the integrity of every transferred file
.Pq an HPN-SSH extension, effective only against an HPN-SSH 19.0 or later target .
After each file is copied, its source and target copies are hashed with XXH3
and compared.
On a terminal
.Nm
prints a one-line completion summary when the transfer finishes \(en
.Dq verified: N files OK ,
or a warning naming how many files failed (the per-file paths are reported as
they occur).
A verification failure sets a nonzero exit status but does not abort the
transfer.
Note the capital letter:
.Fl v
is verbose.
.It Fl Z
Resume an interrupted transfer with integrity verification: only the byte
ranges that differ between the source and target copies are re-sent, and the
repaired file is re-verified.
Implies
.Fl V .
.It Fl X Ar sftp_option
Pass an SFTP-level option
.Pq for example Cm bundle=yes
to the source-to-target transfer.
May be repeated.
.It Fl Y Ar switches
Forward a quoted string of raw
.Xr hpnscp 1
switches to the transfer on the source, for options that
.Nm
does not expose directly.
.El
.Sh AUTHENTICATION
.Nm
runs on the launcher, but the file data moves source-to-target, so it is
the
.Em source
that must authenticate to the target.
.Nm
does this without ever placing your private key on the source:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It source-key-only (default)
The source authenticates to the target with a key of its
.Em own
\(en a key held on the source whose public half is in the target's
.Pa authorized_keys .
Your launcher key never leaves the launcher, and no agent is forwarded.
This is the most contained arrangement and requires no extra flags.
Before any data streams start,
.Nm
runs a preflight check of the source-to-target login; if it fails,
.Nm
stops immediately and prints the fix \(en typically a single
.Xr ssh-copy-id 1
run on the source \(en rather than letting the workers fail one by one.
.It agent forwarding Pq Fl A
If the source has no key of its own on the target, but you can reach the
target yourself,
.Fl A
forwards your
.Xr hpnssh-agent 1
to the source so it can borrow your credentials for the duration of the
transfer.
This is convenient but less contained: while the transfer runs, a
compromised source could use the forwarded keys to authenticate as you
to any host that accepts them.
It is therefore an explicit opt-in, never the default.
.El
.Pp
In both cases the launcher authenticates to the source using your normal
.Xr hpnssh 1
credentials.
Authentication must be non-interactive (public key or a forwarded agent);
.Nm
does not prompt for passwords on the source-to-target hop.
.Sh HOST KEY TRUST
For the source to connect to the target under strict host-key checking,
the source must already trust the target's host key.
When it does not,
.Nm
brokers that trust before launching:
it has the source scan the target's host key, verifies it, and \(en only
on success \(en records it in the source's
.Pa known_hosts .
Verification is fail-closed: the key is accepted only if it is
authenticated by a DNSSEC-signed SSHFP record, or if you confirm its
fingerprint at an interactive prompt.
There is no blind-accept mode.
.Sh OUTPUT
When standard output is a terminal,
.Nm
prints human-readable progress \(en a live meter during the transfer and
plain-text errors \(en to the terminal.
When standard output is redirected or piped,
.Nm
instead emits a line-structured event protocol on standard output,
intended for a driving front end.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev HPN3SCP_REMOTE_HPNSCP
Absolute path to
.Xr hpnscp 1
on the source.
By default
.Nm
discovers it on the source's
.Ev PATH ;
set this when it is installed outside the default search path.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy a file directly from one remote host to another, using four streams:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ hpn3scp -j4 user@src.example.org:/data/big.tar \e
    user@dst.example.org:/incoming/
.Ed
.Pp
Same transfer when the source has no key of its own on the target,
forwarding your agent instead:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ hpn3scp -A -j4 scp://user@src.example.org:2222/data/big.tar \e
    scp://user@dst.example.org:2222/incoming/
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hpnscp 1 ,
.Xr hpnsftp 1 ,
.Xr hpnssh 1 ,
.Xr hpnssh-add 1 ,
.Xr hpnssh-agent 1 ,
.Xr ssh-copy-id 1 ,
.Xr hpnssh_config 5
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in HPN-SSH 19.0.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Chris Rapier Aq Mt rapier@psc.edu
